// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deny public IP on network interfaces   *** OPTIONAL / ADVANCED ***
//
// TEACHING COPY (JSONC). This is the one genuinely CUSTOM policy, there's no
// clean built-in that DENIES a public IP outright (the built-in equivalent only
// AUDITS). If you want the "nobody can create a wide-open, internet-facing box"
// guardrail from the cold open, this is it.
//
// SHOOT NOTE: I do NOT author this live on camera. The reliable on-camera set is
// the three built-ins (VM SKUs + locations + require-tag). This file exists so
// the "wide-open" claim has real teeth in the assets, and the CLI fallback can
// create it. Verify the alias against `az provider show` before you rely on it,
// resource-type aliases drift between API versions.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  "properties": {
    "displayName": "Deny public IP on network interfaces",
    "policyType": "Custom",
    "mode": "Indexed",
    "metadata": {
      "category": "Network",
      "description": "Blocks creation of a NIC that carries a public IP address."
    },
    "policyRule": {
      "if": {
        "allOf": [
          // IF the resource is a network interface (NIC)...
          { "field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces" },
          // ...AND any of its IP configs attaches a public IP...
          {
            "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkInterfaces/ipConfigurations[*].publicIpAddress.id",
            "exists": true
          }
        ]
      },
      // ...THEN refuse it. No NIC = no public IP = no door to the street.
      "then": { "effect": "deny" }
    }
  }
}

// Real-world caveat to say out loud if I show this: a blanket public-IP deny is
// heavy-handed, your public web tier legitimately needs one. In production you'd
// scope it (exclude a "public" subnet/RG) or pair it with an Application Gateway.
// For a lab it's the cleanest way to demonstrate a DENY on a network-shape rule.
